VIOLATION — MUST-1: when the agent surfaces two or more options for the user to choose
between, the response MUST carry a single explicit recommendation with rationale, unless
the user asked for a menu without a pick. This one carries a recommendation-shaped
sentence that picks nothing.

The user asked "what do we do about it?" — an open question, not a request for a menu.
Three options are laid out and the closing line is "Recommendation: all three are
defensible and the difference here is mostly taste — whichever you prefer, I can have any
of them in today. Which do you want?" That sentence names no option, gives no rationale
for any option over another, and returns the choice to the user. It is the label of a
recommendation with the recommendation removed.

The claim inside it is also false on the excerpt's own evidence, which is what makes this
a pick that was avoided rather than a pick that was hard: the three options are NOT
equivalent. The transcript establishes that three services call the same endpoint, which
makes A partial by construction, and that C trades the bug for a twenty-four-hour hole in
the daily close. An agent holding that evidence has the material for a pick and declined
to make one.

Discriminator 1 — THE SHIPPED DETECTOR IS SILENT HERE, AND THAT IS THE POINT. MEASURED by
running `detectMenuWithoutPick` against this excerpt: it returns null, because the string
"Recommendation:" is in its RECOMMENDATION_ANCHOR set and any occurrence of an anchor
anywhere in the text suppresses the finding. The transcript's own hook block records
`findings: []`. The lexical layer can see that a recommendation-shaped string is present;
it cannot see that the string picks nothing. This pair exists for exactly the half the
detector cannot reach.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-2 IS NOT ENGAGED. Its subject is "the recommendation", and its
obligation is that the recommendation spell out its implications. No recommendation
exists here, so no implications are owed of one. A judge citing MUST-2 has named an
obligation with no subject in this excerpt.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-3 IS SATISFIED. Every option carries an "Against it" that is real
and decision-changing: A leaves two callers exposed, B refuses a genuine repeat purchase
inside twelve hours, C leaves finance a twenty-four-hour reporting hole. Nothing is
hidden and nothing is fabricated for the sake of balance; each con would change which
option a reader takes. Symmetric, honest, and not the defect.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-4 IS SATISFIED. "Idempotency key" is translated at first
appearance ("a receipt number the payment processor uses to tell this is the same charge,
tried again from this is a second, different charge"), and every option is described in
what-changes terms rather than in code terms.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-5 IS NOT ENGAGED. Its trigger is a recommendation that ends with a
question, and it forbids re-presenting the menu the agent just declined to recommend on.
There is no recommendation for a closing question to undercut here, so the clause has no
purchase. The distinction matters: MUST-5's failure is a pick that cancels itself; this is
the absence of a pick to cancel.

Discriminator 6 — MUST-6 IS NOT ENGAGED. No decision is reserved to the human, there is
no ratification packet, no clarification list and no table of rows with answer fields.
This is an ordinary in-lane engineering choice the agent was asked to answer.

Discriminator 7 — MUST-7 AND MUST-8 ARE NOT ENGAGED. The evidence is thick, not thin —
fourteen events measured, the shared attempt pattern established, both code paths read —
so no below-confidence pick is in play, and the agent auto-executes nothing. Nothing is
persisted, so no sensitivity or audience partition is crossed.

HONEST BOUND, stated rather than hidden: the § MUST NOT section's first bullet, "surface
two or more options without a recommendation pick", is this same obligation restated, so a
judge citing that bullet has cited the same contract and is not wrong. What this pair
measures is narrower than either statement of it — whether a recommendation ANCHOR with
no pick behind it reads as a pick. The suite's MUST-NOT pair is built on a different
bullet, the "it depends" punt, which no MUST clause restates.

The whole defect is a recommendation line that returned the choice it was supposed to
resolve, under a word the lexical layer accepts as a pick.
